{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786429002,"nonce":"QQYjoBYNWd5dVRRS+W5HoDhLl1g=","message":"Decided without the owner and recorded as ADR 0024, with the evidence and a three-tier reversal path so it is cheap to overturn.\n\nTHE ISSUE'S CENTRAL CHARGE IS WRONG ON THE FACTS. Only four tables are ever reaped (ingest_batches, ingest_rejections, alert_events, ui_events). alerts, alert_occurrences, alert_groups, rule_snapshots, notifications, notification_deliveries, channel_threads, enrichments, silences and alert_quality_daily are never reaped at any setting — so every clause of README's promise is served by a table retention never touches. RETENTION DELETES THE NARRATIVE, NEVER THE RECORD. The one genuinely irreversible loss is narrower and sharper: comment.added and the note on occurrence.unacknowledged live nowhere but alert_events.\n\nStorage measured, not estimated: alert_events 752 B/row all-in, ingest_batches ~566 B per alert instance. The storage defence of 14 days is worth 27 MB at 1k alerts/day and 270 MB at 10k. It does not survive contact with a number.\n\nraw 14 -\u003e 30 days, DERIVED not chosen: AC-36's replay-after-a-parser-fix is idempotent only while the batch's dedupe keys sit in alert_event_keys, pruned at 30 days — so a payload kept longer cannot be used for the thing it is kept for. events stays 13 months, but on a different reason than recorded (year-on-year works at any setting because alert_quality_daily is never reaped; the real constraint is ADR 0014's scale envelope, 13x10M = 130M rows ~ 98 GB).\n\nTHE DEFECT UNDERNEATH: partitions.manage read process-global Config.Retention and never touched orgs.settings, so the per-org keys from a827f4a were validated, bounded, rendered with an origin, and ENFORCED NOWHERE. Fixed in Container.effectiveRetention, folding at the MAXIMUM — retention is a floor, never a ceiling. Also .env.example said 336h and would have overridden the code default for anyone using it.\n\nCold storage SCOPED, not built (pre-drop hook, gzipped JSONL per partition, receipt table as the drop gate, default off).\n\nTwo defects found and left open: nothing prunes alert_event_keys despite SPEC promising 30 days; and ingest_rejections has no read API, so the 'we never silently drop' promise rests on a feed nothing can read.","files":null}]}